Problem 5.2: Calculate the molar conductivity of AgI at zero concentration if the molar conductivities of NaI, AgNO&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; and NaNO&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; at zero concentration are respectively, 126.9, 133.4 and 121.5 \Omega^{-1} cm&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt; mol&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt;.
Solution:
According to Kohrausch law,
i. \wedge_0 (\text{NaI}) = \lambda^0_{\text{Na}} + \lambda^0_{\text{I}}
= 126.9 \Omega^{-1} \text{ cm}^2 \text{ mol}^{-1}
ii. \wedge_0 (\text{AgNO}_3) = \lambda^0_{\text{Ag}^{\oplus}} + \lambda^0_{\text{NO}_3} \ominus
= 133.4 \Omega^{-1} \text{ cm}^2 \text{ mol}^{-1}
iii. \wedge_0 (\text{NaNO}_3) = \lambda^0_{\text{Na}\oplus} + \lambda^0_{\text{NO}_3}\ominus
= 121.5 \Omega^{-1} \text{ cm}^2 \text{ mol}^{-1}
\wedge_0(\text{NaI}) + \wedge_0(\text{AgNO}_3) - \wedge_0(\text{NaNO}_3)
=\lambda^0_{\phantom{0}Na^{\oplus}}+\phantom{0}\lambda^0_{\phantom{0}l\odot}+\lambda^0_{\phantom{0}NO_3\odot}+\lambda^0_{\phantom{0}NO_3\odot}-\lambda^0_{\phantom{0}NO_3\odot}-\lambda^0_{\phantom{0}NO_3\odot}
= \lambda^0_{\ Ag^{\scriptsize \oplus}} + \ \lambda^0_{\ I^{\scriptsize \circleddash}}
= \wedge_{0}(AgI)
= 126.9 \ \Omega^{-1} \ \text{cm}^2 \ \text{mol}^{-1} + 133.4 \ \Omega^{-1} \ \text{cm}^2 \ \text{mol}^{-1} - 121.5 \ \Omega^{-1} \ \text{cm}^2 \ \text{mol}^{-1}
= 138.8 \ \Omega^{-1} \ cm^2 \ mol^{-1}
