Problem 5.3: Calculate molar conductivities at zero concentration for CaCl_2 and Na_2SO_4. Given: molar ionic conductivitis of Ca^{2\theta}, Cl^{\circ}, Na^{\oplus} and SO_4^{2\Theta} ions are respectively, 104, 76.4, 50.1 and 159.6 \Omega^{-1} cm&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt; mol&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt;.
Solution:
According to Kohrausch law,
i. \wedge_0 (CaCl_2) = \lambda_{Ca^{2\oplus}}^0 + 2\lambda_{Cl_2}^0
= 104 \Omega^{-1} \text{ cm}^2 \text{ mol}^{-1} + 2 \times 76.4 \Omega^{-1} \text{ cm}^2 \text{ mol}^{-1}
= 256.8 \Omega^{-1} \text{ cm}^2 \text{ mol}^{-1}
ii. \wedge_0 (N\alpha_2 SO_4) = 2\lambda_{N\alpha}^0 + \lambda_{SO_4}^0
= 2 \times 50.1 \ \Omega^{-1} \ cm^2 \ mol^{-1}
+ 159.6 \Omega^{-1} \text{ cm}^2 \text{ mol}^{-1}
= 259.8 \Omega^{-1} \text{ cm}^2 \text{ mol}^{-1}
